\DOC LOCAL_SQR_4_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SQR_4_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-code goal across one inlined 4-word
modular squaring subroutine, replacing the current symbolic state with the next
one and recording the squaring result as new assumptions.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} offset/index used to locate the call site; the
second is a {string list} of two names identifying the destination and source
operand locations of the squaring step. The conclusion's final state variable
is replaced by a fresh successor state, and the whole block of assumptions
describing the old state (program-counter value, {bytes_loaded}, register and
memory reads, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation) is discharged and
re-added in terms of the new state, with the {MAYCHANGE} term extended by the
components the step may clobber; the program-counter assumption for the new
state names a larger offset. Two new assumptions about the freshly introduced
result value z1 are added, bounding it below twice the modulus and asserting
that it is congruent to the square of the source value modulo that modulus.
Mathematical side conditions already present in {A} (bounds, congruences,
definitional equations) are carried over unchanged.
{
   A ?- eventually ... s_n
   ========================================  LOCAL_SQR_4_TAC n ["dest"; "src"]
   A' ?- eventually ... s_(n+1)
}
\noindent where {A'} is {A} with the old state's assumption block replaced by
the corresponding assumptions for {s_(n+1)} (extended {MAYCHANGE}, larger
program-counter offset) together with the two new assumptions about z1.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces, only this single kind of transformation occurs, always
on goals of this {eventually} form carrying a symbolic-state assumption block.

\ENDDOC
